<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Mamadou Sangaré £5.5m</h3>


<img decoding="async" src="https://thumbs.smartframe.io/598d587f82f0613dc60f89d2daf94a61/brfcMqdBqxlx.webp?force-scraper=rss&#038;t=rss" alt="SmartFrame Image" />


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Another player in the £5.5m bracket that I want to discuss is Brentford&#8217;s new man, Mamadou Sangaré, and this is probably one of the more under-the-radar options in this article. The 24-year-old joined the Bees from RC Lens for a reported £41.1m, which represents a club-record fee for Brentford, so it is clear they see him as a very important player for the future.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sangaré scored 3 goals and provided 4 assists in 29 starts for RC Lens in Ligue 1 last season, but it is his defensive numbers that really catch my eye from an FPL perspective. He averaged 1<strong>4.2 DEFCON per 90 minutes, which suggests he could be something of a DEFCON magnet in the Premier League</strong>. He was also ranked <strong>5th among midfielders in Europe&#8217;s top 5 leagues for ball recoveries per 90 last season</strong>, highlighting just how active he is in midfield.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">He has already hit the ground running for Brentford too, scoring a lovely half-volley in their most recent friendly against Frankfurt.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;ll be completely honest, Sangaré is a player I didn&#8217;t know a huge amount about before he made the move to the Premier League, and I think that is probably the case for a lot of FPL managers. That could explain why he is currently selected by fewer than 2% of managers, making him a really interesting differential.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Of course, there is always a risk when bringing in a player from another league. Ligue 1 to the Premier League is a big step up, and there is always that question of whether you want to trust a new signing in your Gameweek 1 team before seeing how they adapt to English football. However, Sangaré arrives in England with some impressive achievements behind him. Last season, he was named in the Ligue 1 Team of the Season, won the Prix Marc-Vivien Foé as the league&#8217;s best African player, helped Lens finish 2nd in Ligue 1 and was part of the side that won the Coupe de France for the first time in the club&#8217;s history.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For me, he is definitely one to watch. I am not quite ready to say he is a must-have for Gameweek 1, especially given the step up to the Premier League, but at £5.5m, his defensive numbers make him a very interesting option.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If he can translate that form to Brentford, he could become exactly the sort of budget midfielder I am looking for.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Further Read: <a href="https://allaboutfpl.com/2026/08/2026-27-fpl-chip-strategy-guide-first-half-of-the-season/" data-type="link" data-id="https://allaboutfpl.com/2026/08/2026-27-fpl-chip-strategy-guide-first-half-of-the-season/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"><mark style="background-color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)" class="has-inline-color has-vivid-red-color">FPL Chip Strategy Guide- Best Windows to Play Free Hit, Wildcard, Triple Captain and Bench Boost</mark></a></strong></p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Diego Gómez £5.0m</h3>


<img decoding="async" src="https://thumbs.smartframe.io/c78749ece7fd074ea5dc1889e7c53f0f/esmeCnFsWgmh.webp?force-scraper=rss&#038;t=rss" alt="SmartFrame Image" />


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The final part of this article moves into the £5.0m and £4.5m options, and unfortunately, at the time of writing, there isn&#8217;t loads to write home about.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The standout £5.0m option for me at the moment is Brighton&#8217;s Diego Gómez. He has registered 1<strong> assist in pre-season, while last season he scored 5 goals and provided 1 assist.</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Gómez started on the wing in Brighton&#8217;s most recent pre-season friendly and looked like a strong candidate to start Gameweek 1, particularly with Mitoma and Minteh dealing with fitness and injury concerns.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">However, there is now a slight concern over Gómez himself. He was substituted in that most recent friendly and was seen limping down the tunnel, so this is definitely a situation we need to monitor over the next few days. Before that, I thought Gómez was probably the front-runner from the £5.0m midfielders, purely because he looked like he could offer both attacking potential and regular minutes.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If he is fit for Gameweek 1, he could still be a really interesting option, particularly with Brighton&#8217;s attacking options being affected by injuries. For now, though, I think we need to wait for an update on his fitness before getting too excited. At £5.0m, he could be a very useful budget option, but there is no point picking a £5.0m midfielder if he&#8217;s going to be watching the first Gameweek from the physio room!</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Ethan Ampadu £5.5m</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Ampadu is a nailed starter for Leeds and is a DEFCON magnet who averaged 11.89 Defensive Contributions/90 last season. He is also another option to consider for the 5.5 million Midfielder slot. </p>



<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Top Midfielders for DEFCON last season:</h4>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Anderson- 514</li>



<li>Garner- 458</li>



<li><strong>Ampadu- 412</strong></li>



<li>Scott- 386</li>



<li>Rice- 377</li>
</ul>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading"> Christian Nørgaard £5.0m &amp; James Garner £6.0m (Monitor)</h3>


<div class="wp-block-image">
<figure class="aligncenter size-full is-resized"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="542" height="273" src="https://cdn.allaboutfpl.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Screenshot-2026-08-17-152124.png" alt="" class="wp-image-84958" style="width:840px;height:auto" srcset="https://cdn.allaboutfpl.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Screenshot-2026-08-17-152124.png 542w, https://cdn.allaboutfpl.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Screenshot-2026-08-17-152124-300x151.png 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 542px) 100vw, 542px" /></figure>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">One player I would avoid for Gameweek 1 at the moment, but definitely keep an eye on, is Everton&#8217;s new signing Christian Nørgaard. Priced at just £5.0m, I think Nørgaard could eventually become a really good value option. However, he has missed Everton&#8217;s last 2 friendlies due to a lack of match fitness, so I wouldn&#8217;t be looking to bring him into my squad just yet.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Nørgaard is obviously no stranger to the Premier League, having spent several seasons at Brentford before moving to Arsenal. Things didn&#8217;t quite work out for him at Arsenal, with opportunities and minutes limited, but I think this move to Everton could give him the chance to establish himself as an important player again. For me, he is more of a player to monitor over the opening few Gameweeks. If he starts getting regular minutes and looks like he is going to be a consistent starter for David Moyes, then £5.0m could look like a very attractive price. I would expect him to become a regular starter for Everton once he is fully fit, so this could be one to revisit once we have a better idea of his minutes.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">While we are on Everton, James Garner (£6.0m) is another player I would keep an eye on. He is currently injured, so for me he is a definite avoid for Gameweek 1 unless David Moyes gives us some very positive news on his fitness over the next few days.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Both Nørgaard and Garner are players I would keep on the watchlist rather than considering for my Gameweek 1 team. Moyes will want to get both back into his side as soon as they are ready, and if Nørgaard can establish himself as a regular starter, I could see £5.0m looking like an absolute steal later in the season.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Further Read:</strong> <strong><a href="https://allaboutfpl.com/2026/07/fpl-rotational-pairs-combinations-for-the-2026-27-fpl-season/" data-type="link" data-id="https://allaboutfpl.com/2026/07/fpl-rotational-pairs-combinations-for-the-2026-27-fpl-season/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"><mark style="background-color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)" class="has-inline-color has-vivid-red-color">FPL Rotational Pairs and Combinations for 2026/27 FPL Season</mark></a></strong></p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">The £4.5m <strong>Budget FPL Midfielder</strong>s (Slater, Yates and Hughes)</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Finally, we move into the £4.5m bracket, and if I&#8217;m being completely honest, I really can&#8217;t see a huge amount of potential here at the moment.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The player I would probably lean towards is Hull City&#8217;s Regan Slater.</p>


<img decoding="async" src="https://thumbs.smartframe.io/1ffbae35d432d0d71911ca2a0b209fbb/prsikrQ3fPU4.webp?force-scraper=rss&#038;t=rss" alt="SmartFrame Image" />


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">He is expected to start for Hull in the opening Gameweek or two, which makes him an interesting option at £4.5m. However, Hull are continuing to make a number of signings, so while I think Slater looks likely to get some early minutes, I wouldn&#8217;t say he is nailed on to remain a regular starter for the long term.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Slater scored 2 goals and provided 2 assists in the Championship last season as Hull secured promotion back to the Premier League. However, I wouldn&#8217;t expect huge attacking returns from him at £4.5m. His main appeal is simply the possibility of regular minutes at the cheapest possible price.</p>


<div class="wp-block-image">
<figure class="aligncenter size-full is-resized"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="536" height="264" src="https://cdn.allaboutfpl.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Screenshot-2026-08-17-152645.png" alt="" class="wp-image-84959" style="aspect-ratio:2.0303340010629163;width:840px;height:auto" srcset="https://cdn.allaboutfpl.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Screenshot-2026-08-17-152645.png 536w, https://cdn.allaboutfpl.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Screenshot-2026-08-17-152645-300x148.png 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 536px) 100vw, 536px" /></figure>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Other names that have been mentioned around the £4.5m bracket include Nottingham Forest&#8217;s Ryan Yates and Crystal Palace&#8217;s Will Hughes, both of whom currently have higher ownership than Slater.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">But if I&#8217;m being completely honest, I don&#8217;t think there is much between the 3 of them. For me, unfortunately, none of these options are quite convincing enough from a minutes or points perspective to secure a place in my Gameweek 1 team.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you are looking for a £4.5m midfielder purely to sit on your bench, then Slater is probably the one I would be leaning towards at the moment. However, I certainly wouldn&#8217;t be going out of my way to find a reason to put any of them in my starting XI.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Further Read:</strong><a href="https://allaboutfpl.com/2026/08/2026-27-pre-season-summary-of-all-20-premier-league-teams/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"><mark style="background-color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)" class="has-inline-color has-vivid-red-color"><strong>2026/27 Pre-Season Summary of All 20 Premier League Teams</strong></mark></a></p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">My Budget FPL Midfielders watchlist</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Before I wrap things up, there are a few other players in this price bracket that I haven&#8217;t mentioned in as much detail, but are certainly worth keeping an eye on during pre-season and the opening few Gameweeks.</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Tottenham&#8217;s new signings, Mateus Fernandes and Sandro Tonali, have both already found the back of the net in pre-season and could be interesting options if they nail down regular starting places.</li>



<li>At £6.0m, Liverpool&#8217;s Ryan Gravenberch is another player worth mentioning. I suspect most managers will prefer to spend the extra £1.0m on Dominik Szoboszlai, but Gravenberch does offer a cheaper route into what should be one of the better attacking teams in the league.</li>



<li>Caleb Yirenkyi is another option on the watchlist; he scored <strong>2 Goals, 6 Assists, 0.19 xGI/90 in 30 apps</strong> for Nordsjælland in the Danish Superliga. Most importantly, he averaged <strong>13.05 DEFCON/90</strong>. In Coventry&#8217;s last friendly, he got an assist and got 9 DEFCON&#8217;s in 65 minutes (via <strong><a href="https://x.com/AlexVolloFPL" data-type="link" data-id="https://x.com/AlexVolloFPL" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"><mark style="background-color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)" class="has-inline-color has-vivid-red-color">@AlexVolloFPL</mark></a></strong>)</li>



<li>There are also a couple of Aston Villa players I want to keep an eye on. John McGinn (£5.5m) has already registered 1 goal and 1 assist in pre-season, and I think he could have an even more important role this season. Morgan Rogers, Youri Tielemans and Lucas Digne have all left the club, and while they don&#8217;t all play in the same position as McGinn, all 3 were regular starters for Villa last season. With those experienced players now gone, I would expect Unai Emery to turn to McGinn as one of the senior figures in the squad. I expect him to start most games and play an important role in helping Villa maintain the standards they have shown over the last couple of seasons despite losing some key players.</li>



<li>And finally, Emiliano Buendía (£6.0m) is one I will definitely be watching. He has already scored 2 goals and provided 4 assists in pre-season, and we know there is plenty of ability there. If he can find some consistency and secure regular minutes, he could become a very interesting option.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I probably wouldn&#8217;t pick any of these ahead of the players I&#8217;ve discussed in more detail, but that&#8217;s not to say they should be ignored. Early gameweeks can change things very quickly, so these are definitely names I will be keeping on my watchlist.</p>



